    {
      "id": "mcp3008",
      "name": "MCP3008 8-channel ADC",
      "category": "Analog IC",
      "subcategory": "ADCs",
      "description": "10-bit, 8-channel SPI analog-to-digital converter, DIP-16.",
      "keywords": ["mcp3008", "adc", "spi", "10-bit", "8 channel"],
      "protocols": ["SPI"],
      "part_number": "MCP3008",
      "pins": [
        {"name": "CH0", "type": "analog"}, {"name": "CH1", "type": "analog"}, {"name": "CH2", "type": "analog"}, {"name": "CH3", "type": "analog"},
        {"name": "CH4", "type": "analog"}, {"name": "CH5", "type": "analog"}, {"name": "CH6", "type": "analog"}, {"name": "CH7", "type": "analog"},
        {"name": "DGND", "type": "ground"}, {"name": "CS", "type": "input"}, {"name": "DIN", "type": "input"},
        {"name": "DOUT", "type": "output"}, {"name": "CLK", "type": "clock"}, {"name": "AGND", "type": "ground"},
        {"name": "VREF", "type": "power"}, {"name": "VDD", "type": "power"}
      ],
      "model": {"shape": "dip16"},
      "mounting": "tht",
      "simulation_status": "external",
      "simulation_models": ["spi-adc-probe"],
      "simulation_backend": "firmware",
      "arduino_compatible": true,
      "params": {
        "vref": {"default": 5.0, "unit": "V", "label": "Reference voltage"}
      },
      "notes": "No breadboard electrical model of its own: the harness serves it on the ATmega's SPI peripheral (so a sketch must use the hardware SPI pins - DIN = D11, CLK = D13 - and a bit-banged bus is invisible), and the part *answers* on MISO, which makes it the first SPI device firmware can read. A conversion is the datasheet's three transfers inside one CS-low window (0x01, then 0x80 | channel << 4, then a don't-care) and the 10-bit code comes back in the low bits of the second byte and all of the third. CH0..CH7 are ADC probes on the solved nets: attach_devices feeds each channel its solved node voltage (the same closed loop as the PCF8591), scaled to a code by 'vref', so a divider or sensor output the circuit solves can be read by firmware. CS must be wired to a virtual pin and mapped with pin_map ({\"10\": \"P2\"}) - without it the harness cannot tell where a conversion begins, so the part is skipped rather than guessed at. Not modelled: differential mode (a differential request is answered with the channel's single-ended code and counted), the VREF *net* (the reference is the 'vref' parameter), conversion timing/throughput, and the analog input's own loading."
    },
